      "result": "All 19 checklist items have been tested and recorded — coverage is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\n**17 PASS / 2 FAIL**\n\nCore to-do functionality (create, edit, complete, delete, active/completed views, counts, priority & deadline sorting, empty-title rejection) and the dependency system (prerequisite assignment, blocked-state distinction, warning labels, cycle prevention, dashed-line connectors with dependency-chain tooltips) all behave correctly.\n\n### Failures\n\n**FT-3 — Blocked tasks are entirely uneditable.**\nThe requirement is that a blocked task still allows editing its *description* and *priority*, while locking title and deadline. In practice the Edit button is fully `disabled` on every blocked task, so the form can never be opened. Enumerating all interactive elements inside a blocked card yields only complete (disabled), edit (disabled) and delete (enabled) — there is no inline or alternative path to those two fields.\n\n**CS-22 — Reopening a prerequisite creates a contradictory, unrecoverable state.**\nAfter completing \"Review project proposal\" then its dependent \"Complete tax filing\", reopening the prerequisite was permitted without returning the dependent to an incomplete state. \"Complete tax filing\" now sits in the Completed view (counted in \"Completed 3\", toggle state `completed`) while simultaneously showing the blocked warning *Prerequisite \"Review project proposal\" incomplete*. Worse, because blocking also disables its complete toggle, the task is **stuck** — it can no longer be reopened, only deleted.\n\n### Noted but not failed\nThe long dashed connector's 16px transparent hit path overlays a shorter connector segment, so hovering that shorter segment surfaces the long connector's dependency chain rather than its own. IX-18's requirement is still met, so this is recorded as a caveat rather than a failure.",
